#{[17592186139294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1061 165169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1061 165170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1061 165171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1061 165174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1061 165177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139305 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139305 #edb/ref 1061 17374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be8f78e1-4021-5706-815d-35b712aee1e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139306 #edb/ref 1061 17375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16b49c7f-043e-5aeb-9d10-cd74f7f3ddf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139307 #edb/ref 1061 17376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d5b8d5-8593-5e6a-84da-d4aaea527af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139326 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139326 #edb/ref 1061 18177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e0565a6-cf26-5e4b-8bc5-c3de10c211d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139644 #edb/ref 1064 "To divide square roots, we simply divide the numbers under the root. This is called the **quotient rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} }$ by applying the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt{2} } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt{5}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[3]{5}\n$$\n\nIn general, the quotient rule works for any quotient of $n$th roots. So for any $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{10} }{ \\sqrt[n]{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac{10}{2} } = \\sqrt[n]{5}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the quotient rule when we have the quotient of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\dfrac{ \\sqrt{10} }{ \\sqrt[3]{5} }$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$ are different." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139644 #edb/ref 1065 3297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139644 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ea49118-4345-575a-a14f-9aedade211d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139644 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1064 "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1065 8751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1065 8752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1067 "The Greatest Possible Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186178724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178720 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1c7a255b-8fbd-55cd-be4c-153c5fe41cd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178721 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178721 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178721 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f24ac7c-d545-5f8f-8ab4-e001f44e5d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178722 #edb/ref 1080 "36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1697916d-04a7-5927-b549-9b41576aa948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178723 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178723 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178723 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "37667bb2-d570-5cc1-8a08-21c257e3c558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178724 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3809