ref 17592186045417] [17592186179862 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179862 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5548d9d0-335e-5dab-afd6-a236e37418d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Percentage Given Two Numbers: Word Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "39af6225-7719-5f09-ae8c-6df89ff6b7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d92da08-8917-5c11-931f-ed827ad0de00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180196 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}={(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180620 #edb/ref 1061 62339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72c8ed91-9fb7-5d29-a50e-eaf59de22272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180621 #edb/ref 1061 62340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "824c9688-1516-55a2-af62-bf4fc82a884b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180622 #edb/ref 1061 62343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c6ef7dd-08af-5926-982f-7fef0db63de0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1061 7964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bdf65be2-4038-5c62-b8e0-2362d10939cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1061 7965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a3797e0-441d-5d87-adba-4fa2a33a82b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1061 7966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0c640ea-bd66-5449-a695-d9913915d65f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1064 "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} ,\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}.\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1065 2082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "73bca8be-ae46-5214-9ba6-2beb553be49f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180668 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify the product of two square roots, such as $\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}.$\nFirst, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 = 3.\n$$\n\nWe can do something similar for a product of three cube roots, like $\\sqrt[3]{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3}.$\nFirst, we rewrite the expression as a cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\left(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\right)^3 = 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180668 #edb/ref 1065 3100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180668 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "237a4f95-3604-56a2-8809-03b4d40f35d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180668 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187776 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187776 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187776 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759